<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hassan Gouled Aptidon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1916">1916</a>–<a href="/years/2006">2006</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hassan Gouled Aptidon was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/issa-clan">Issa Somali</a> politician who represented French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-somaliland">Somaliland</a> in the French parliament and later led the campaign for independence. He became the first president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djibouti">Djibouti</a> at independence in 1977 and ruled until 1999. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-rally-for-progress">People's Rally for Progress</a> became the only legal party in 1981, and his rule faced an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afar">Afar</a> rebellion from 1991. His nephew Ismaïl Omar Guelleh succeeded him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hassan-gouled-aptidon.jpg" alt="Hassan Gouled Aptidon" width="216" height="273" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hassan_Gouled_Aptidon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hassan Gouled Aptidon was born on 15 October 1916 in the small village of Gerisa in the Lughaya district of British Somaliland, into the Mamassan subset of the Issa clan. He began his career in the 1930s as a nurse and was stationed at Dikhil in 1932, and was later an entrepreneur. In 1946 he founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> and Dankali Youth Club with Mahamoud Harbi Farah. They were elected together to the colony's Representative Council in 1951, then separated, and Gouled was elected French senator against Harbi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Gouled sat in the French Senate from 1952 to 1958 and campaigned against Harbi, who sought to join the territory to neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a>. He was vice-president of the Government Council from 1958 to April 1959, and a member of the French National Assembly from 1959 to 1962, when he lost his seat. He led the Democratic Union Issa in the 1960s, was education minister under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-aref-bourhan">Ali Aref Bourhan</a> from 1963 to 1967 and was briefly jailed in July 1967. Elected to the Territorial Assembly in 1968, he later became Minister of the Interior.</p><p>In 1974 he called a vote with the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francois-mitterrand">François Mitterrand</a>. He became president of the African People's League for Independence, created in February 1975, and was President of the Governing Council on 18 May 1977. The Chamber of Deputies elected him President of the future Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djibouti">Djibouti</a> on 28 May 1977.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>After an attack on the Zinc Palm in December 1977 he banned the main opposition party and set up a one-party system. In 1981 he declared his party, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-rally-for-progress">People's Rally for Progress</a>, the only legal one, and was elected without opposition on 12 June 1981 with 84.58 per cent of the vote. The Djiboutian Civil War began in 1991. A referendum in September 1992 allowed four parties, and in the December 1992 elections the People's Rally won all 65 seats. He was re-elected for a fourth term in May 1993 with 60.7 per cent.</p><p>On 4 February 1999 he announced his retirement. His nephew Ismaïl Omar Guelleh, who had increasingly handled affairs for him, was chosen as the party's candidate, won the April 1999 election and succeeded him on 8 May 1999. Gouled Aptidon died at his home on 21 November 2006, aged 90.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hassan Gouled Aptidon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Hassan Gouled Aptidon</b>, an Issa Somali politician, became its first president.</blockquote><a href="/state/djibouti">Djibouti</a></li><li><blockquote>Nationalists such as <b>Hassan Gouled Aptidon</b> pressed for independence, and in 1976 a hostage crisis at Loyada drew world attention.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-territory-of-the-afars-and-the-issas">French Territory of the Afars and the Issas</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Lughaya District. Died in Djibouti. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=10.65,43.58&amp;year=1974" aria-label="Lughaya District on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Hassan Gouled Aptidon"><image href="/maps/globes/g03000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6131 4095h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6131 4095h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=10.65,43.58&amp;year=1974">What was here in 1974? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
